{
  "dom_id": "step-q182624",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=2}^{n}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}n(n-1)(n+1)\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=2}^{2}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}{{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=2}^{k}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=2}^{k+1}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}{{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=2}^{n}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}n(n-1)(n+1)$ for all $n\\ge 2.$",
  "worked_solution": "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=2}^{n}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}n(n-1)(n+1)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case,** $P(2).$ To do this, we substitute $n=2$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=2}^{2}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}(2)(2-1)(2+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 2.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$ \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=2}^{k}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}k(k-1)(k+1).\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=2,$ so this assumption is valid. \n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$ \n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=2}^{k+1}i(i-1) & =\\frac{1}{3}(k+1)((k+1)-1)((k+1)+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}k(k+1)(k+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=2}^{n}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}n(n-1)(n+1)$ for all $n\\ge 2.$",
